// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Zerg Rush (Quantity Over Quality · Death by a Thousand Cuts of bodies)
// @category Military and Warfare Tropes
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Zerg_Rush
// @domain   Interpersonal
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): defeat a stronger opponent with a very large number of cheap, disposable
// combatants — quantity over quality, named for the StarCraft Zerg who swamp a better foe under a
// tide of weak, expendable units. The signature this file recognizes is the TACTIC of winning by
// NUMBERS, not skill: a [+Horde] of [+Expendable] units swarms a [+Stronger] / [+Outnumbered]
// target, and it is the sheer COUNT of attackers crashing in — not any one decisive blow — that
// overwhelms the target. Rides the action-dynamics Strikes vocabulary, counted: a high tally of
// strikes against one target swamps it. Distinct from We Have Reserves (the COMMANDER's callous
// attitude — that he spends his own as fuel and it costs him nothing): Zerg Rush is the assault
// itself, won by quantity. Distinct from a Last Stand (a few HOLD against the many) and a
// Curb-Stomp (a stronger party routs a weaker): here the WEAKER-per-unit side wins, by bodies.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict
import "trl/modules/action_dynamics.trl"   // Strikes — the swarm of attacks, counted, against one target

assoc zerg_rush -> [
  action_dynamics  : "a counted flood of Strikes in",
  conflict         : "wins by numbers, not skill, a",
  we_have_reserves : "the tactic to the commander's callous attitude in",
  siege            : "an assault-by-mass rather than the slow encirclement of",
  curb_stomp_battle : "the underdog-per-unit inverse of the rout in",
  last_stand       : "the swarm that finally swamps a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// Quantity over quality: the win is the count of expendable bodies, not any single blow's force.
imply ZergRush -> [Attrition, Swarm, Tactic, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: a [+Stronger] / [+Outnumbered] target swarmed by a [+Horde] of [+Expendable] units —
// recognized by the COUNT of strikes crashing in on that one target (five or more: a tide, not a
// duel) — is swamped: [+Overwhelmed] by numbers. The count() clause is the trope's whole signature
// and what separates it from a single Strike or Finisher: no one blow is decisive; the mass is. The
// target's per-unit superiority ([+Stronger]) is precisely what is overrun, so the win reads as
// quantity-over-quality. (Who throws the bodies and why they're disposable is We Have Reserves; here
// the recognition is the assault that wins by drowning the foe.)
rule Zerg_Rush {
  when:
    char $horde  [+Horde] [+Expendable]
    char $target [+Outnumbered]
    count( evt $rush [&Strikes(agent=$horde, target=$target)] ) >= 5
  then:
    $target [+Swamped]
    $target [+Overwhelmed]
    $horde [+ZergRush]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: StarCraft — a zergling rush on a terran base (real-time strategy game, 1998) ===
// The eponym. The Zerg pour cheap zerglings out of the hatchery faster than they can be killed and
// send them swarming across the map at a terran outpost before its defenders are ready. Any single
// zergling is trash — a marine outclasses it one-on-one — but they don't come one at a time. Wave
// after wave crashes into the chokepoint; the bunker's fire drops a dozen and a dozen more pour
// over the bodies, until the line is simply swamped under claws. The base falls not to a better
// unit but to more units than it can shoot. Quantity over quality, the trope's namesake play.

// THE ZERG SWARM (the cheap, endless zerglings)
char swarm   [+Antagonist] [+Collective] [+Horde] [+Expendable]
// THE TERRAN OUTPOST GARRISON (better troops, too few of them)
char garrison [+Protagonist] [+Collective] [+Outnumbered]
garrison [+Stronger]                        // per-unit superior — and precisely what gets overrun
// EXT. THE OUTPOST CHOKEPOINT — THE RAMP — DAY
set  chokepoint [+Exterior] [+Day] [+Battlefield]

scene the_rush {
  beat 1 {
    swarm    @ chokepoint
    garrison @ chokepoint
  }
  beat 2 { evt wave_one   [&Strikes(agent=swarm, target=garrison)] }
  beat 3 { evt wave_two   [&Strikes(agent=swarm, target=garrison)] }
  beat 4 { evt wave_three [&Strikes(agent=swarm, target=garrison)] }
  beat 5 { evt wave_four  [&Strikes(agent=swarm, target=garrison)] }
  beat 6 {
    evt wave_five [&Strikes(agent=swarm, target=garrison)]
    // Zerg_Rush fires: count(&Strikes agent=swarm target=garrison) >= 5
    //   -> garrison [+Swamped] [+Overwhelmed];  swarm [+ZergRush]   (not one blow — the mass)
  }
}
